extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Styling | Background"
	- pagedescription = "The background colour in Tablechart, which lighter lines and donut slices are mixed with."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Background
				p.lead The colour that lighter lines and slices are mixed with.

	+section("What uses the background colour")
		p #[code --tablechart-surface] is the background colour of a chart. Each line after the first is lighter than the one before it, and so is each donut slice. The lighter colour is the line or slice colour mixed with #[code --tablechart-surface].
		p If you set a colour for each line or slice with a #[a(href=url('/styling/colours.html')) palette], the background colour is not used.
		p These parts are cut out of the chart, so they show the real background. You do not need to set anything for them:
		ul
			li the inside of open points on a line;
			li the callout of a bracket or trend arrow;
			li the gaps between donut slices;
			li the break in a scale.

	+section("Set it")
		p By default, #[code --tablechart-surface] is the background the browser uses for the page: white in light mode and dark in dark mode. See #[a(href=url('/styling/light-dark.html')) Light and dark]. If your page or a part of it has another background, set #[code --tablechart-surface] to that colour.

		+style.
			.dark {
				background: #16323f;
				color: #f3efe9;
				padding: 1em;
				--tablechart-surface: #16323f;
				--tablechart-slice-color: #f3efe9;
			}

		+chart
			figure.dark(data-chart="donut")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Juiced', '38.2'], ['Eaten fresh', '18.1'], ['Sold whole', '12.4'], ['Composted', '5.8']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
